Bringing a one-page syllabus is reasonable if you present it as a **discussion draft**, not a decision already made. His role as room coordinator may give him logistical authority, but it does not by itself establish authority over the group’s intellectual or devotional scope.

Use a neutral title such as **“Proposed Session Guidelines—Discussion Draft.”** Keep it focused on process:

- purpose: civic-history study;
- scheduled topics and readings;
- religious sources studied historically rather than devotionally;
- readings circulated at least 7 days in advance;
- substitutions agreed in advance;
- opening presentations limited to a stated time;
- changes decided by the group.

A suitable opening is:

> I wanted to discuss the format before the next meeting. I drafted one page so we would have something concrete to react to; it isn’t a finished policy. My aim is to keep the sessions focused on civic history and make any changes to readings predictable.

First describe the concern verbally, then hand him the draft. That makes the document support the conversation rather than replace it.

One relevant unknown is whether use of the chapel hall comes with conditions concerning religious programming. Check any room agreement or host expectations before proposing language that could conflict with the venue’s rules. Those conditions should be distinguished from the coordinator’s personal preferences.

**Recommendation:** bring the draft, label it explicitly as a proposal, and seek group adoption rather than asking the coordinator alone to approve it.
